<script>
' This app's root Scene, compiled from .thr like every other component in this app — see
' apps/focus-demo/src/components/MainScene.brs for the deliberately-kept-old-style counterpart (hand-
' composed BrightScript, no template of its own) that proves the two architectures interoperate.
' See GRAMMAR.md's "`<component>` — the mandatory root tag" section for what makes a Scene-rooted
' .thr component possible at all, and its "Router" section for what this file demonstrates.
'
' Test harness for the router feature — nested routing with persistent chrome:
'   - "/splash" (the app's own first-ever route, see setup() below) activates SplashScreen.thr — a
'     genuine "nothing has focus yet" vacuum, filled explicitly once its own delayed default-focus
'     element appears; see that file's own top comment and findings/focus-system.md.
'   - "/browse" activates Shell.thr, a persistent menu (wraps FavoriteCounter) that stays mounted
'     across every navigation within "/browse/*" — its own nested FlashTheaterRouterOutlet
'     independently swaps HomeScreen.thr/ScheduleScreen.thr/LoadingDemoScreen.thr/
'     CardsScreen.thr/TaskDemoScreen.thr/StreamDemo.thr/RequestDemoScreen.thr/
'     RouterTransitionDemo.thr/DirectionalFocusDemo.thr/DirectionalFocusDemoDetail.thr inside it.
'     Verify live that navigating "/browse" -> "/browse/schedule" does NOT rebuild Shell/
'     FavoriteCounter (confirms the persistent-chrome mechanism, not just that it compiles) — see
'     findings/router.md.
'   - `router.isBackJourney` + explicit `focus(<id>)` — DirectionalFocusDemo.thr/
'     DirectionalFocusDemoDetail.thr demonstrate choosing a route's own initial focus based on
'     which DIRECTION it was entered from, reached via HomeScreen.thr's own second prompt. See
'     DirectionalFocusDemo.thr's own top comment and findings/router-focus-integration.md.
'   - router.navigate(path, params)/router.navigate(path, params, skipInHistory), router.back(),
'     router.params.*, router.updateBackJourneyData(...) — see HomeScreen.thr/ScheduleScreen.thr
'     for the actual demonstrations. Shell's own childOutlet — navigate-out:/navigate-in:/
'     back-out:/back-in: slide transitions, loadingComponent + router.markReady() — see Shell.thr's
'     own top comment and RouterTransitionDemo.thr for the router-outlet-transitions feature.
'   - default-focus="true" — each screen marks its own natural entry point; a router-mounted
'     component is always a brand-new instance, so there is never a "remembered last focus" to
'     fall back on instead (see FlashTheaterFocusManager.brs's firstRegistrantOfOwner).
'   - The physical/ECP "back" key needs no on:key[back] handler anywhere in this file (or any
'     screen below) — it's the compiler's own generated onKeyEvent fallthrough (see
'     codegen/brs-emitter.ts's emitOnKeyEventFunction) that walks the router's history, and stops
'     consuming the key once history is empty so Roku's own default behavior can exit the app.
'
' Shell itself declares its own focusable sidebar menu (two entries, navigating between "/browse"
' and "/browse/schedule" without ever taking focus WITH it — see Shell.thr's own top comment for
' the vacuum rule this demonstrates) — this used to be impossible for persistent chrome (an earlier
' outlet design would have stolen focus back from whatever a nested outlet's own decision already
' set); see findings/router.md's "Persistent-chrome components must declare no focusable elements
' of their own — restriction removed" entry for the fix that made it safe.

public function setup() {
  m.top.backgroundColor = "0x101010FF"

  ' Must run before Shell's own FavoriteCounter (created moments later, synchronously, as part of
  ' the router.navigate() call right below) ever reads it — same store-seed-before-first-read
  ' ordering concern this app's own original demo already established for the same reason.
  store(favoriteCount) = 0

  router.setRouting([
    { path: "splash", component: "SplashScreen" },
    {
      path: "browse",
      component: "Shell",
      children: [
        { path: "", component: "HomeScreen" },
        { path: "schedule", component: "ScheduleScreen" },
        { path: "loading", component: "LoadingDemoScreen" },
        { path: "cards", component: "CardsScreen" },
        { path: "tasks", component: "TaskDemoScreen" },
        { path: "streams", component: "StreamDemo" },
        { path: "requests", component: "RequestDemoScreen" },
        { path: "transition-demo", component: "RouterTransitionDemo" },
        { path: "directional-focus", component: "DirectionalFocusDemo" },
        { path: "directional-focus-detail", component: "DirectionalFocusDemoDetail" }
      ]
    }
  ])

  ' Triggers the whole outlet-mount-and-focus cascade synchronously, here, AFTER screen.show()
  ' (source/Main.brs calls scene.callFunc("setup") after show(), not before — see that file's own
  ' comment for the real-device hang this order avoids).
  '
  ' Nothing else is needed to establish focus: the compiler emits its own shallow
  ' applyPendingFocus() call as a sibling statement directly after this one (look at the generated
  ' MainScene.brs), which applies whatever the mount cascade proposed. SplashScreen itself proposes
  ' nothing at mount time (its own default-focus element doesn't exist until its Timer fires, well
  ' after this call already returned) — so this specific navigate() leaves a genuine vacuum, and
  ' SplashScreen.thr's own explicit claimFocusIfVacant() call fills it once "Enter app" actually
  ' exists. Deliberately NO m.top.setFocus(true) after this: focusing the Scene itself would leave
  ' the Scene holding focus (it only handles "back"), which real-device testing caught as "no key
  ' press below back reaches any screen". See findings/router.md and runtime-assets/FocusManager's
  ' "Deferred focus application"/claimFocusIfVacant doc comments.
  router.navigate("/splash")
}
</script>

<component extends="Scene">
<FlashTheaterRouterOutlet id="rootOutlet" />
</component>
